  "answer": "The United Kingdom uses a **dual system**: the **metric system (SI)** is the **primary legal system for trade, administration, education, and most official purposes**, while **imperial units remain legally permitted and culturally entrenched for specific uses** (road distances/speed limits in miles/mph, draught beer/cider and milk in pints, precious metals in troy ounces).\n\n**Legally**: The Units of Measurement Regulations 1995 (as amended) implement EU Directive 80/181/EEC, making SI units mandatory for economic, public health, safety, and administrative purposes. Post-Brexit, the UK retained this framework.\n\n**In practice**:\n- **Metric-only**: Most packaged goods, fuel (litres), temperature (°C), medicine, construction, industry, science, education.\n- **Imperial-only (or primary)**: Road signs (miles, yards, mph), draught beer/cider (pints), returnable milk containers (pints), precious metals (troy ounces).\n- **Dual labelling common**: Many products show both (e.g., milk sold in 568 ml / 1 pint containers).\n\nThe UK never fully completed metrication; it is a **metric-majority country with statutory imperial exceptions**.",
  "confidence": 0.98
}

