  "answer": "University tuition was not abolished at a single UK-wide moment; it ended at different times in each nation:\n\n1. **England**: Free tuition for home/EU undergraduates ended in **1998** (Teaching and Higher Education Act 1998), introducing £1,000/year means-tested fees from 1998/99. Fees rose to £3,000 in 2006/07, £9,000 in 2012/13, and £9,250 in 2017/18.\n\n2. **Scotland**: The Graduate Endowment (£2,000 payable after graduation) was introduced in **2001** and abolished in **2008**. Scottish-domiciled students have paid **no upfront tuition fees** since 2008/09 (funded by SAAS).\n\n3. **Wales**: Tuition fees were introduced in **1998** (same UK Act), but from **2007/08** Welsh-domiciled students received a fee grant covering the increase above ~£1,200, and since **2012/13** a full fee grant covers the £9,000/£9,250 fee at any UK university.\n\n4. **Northern Ireland**: Fees introduced in **1998**; since **2006/07** NI-domiciled students pay a capped fee (£4,710 in 2024/25) rather than the full £9,250.\n\n**Key nuance**: \"Free\" historically meant no *upfront* tuition fees (maintenance grants/loans existed separately). The 1998 Act ended the universal free-tuition regime for England, Wales, and NI; Scotland followed a distinct path, restoring free-at-point-of-entry tuition in 2008.",
  "confidence": 0.97
}

